"Tool",
"Meter reading V (kV)",
"Electron Diffraction and de Broglie Waves | VirtualDemos",
"Electron Diffraction and de Broglie Waves",
"Student laboratory · Physics II and Modern Physics",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (and §{#}). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Low voltage, big rings; Raise the voltage; Two rings, two spacings; Is it the electrons?; Measure the spacings; Why only two rings.",
"A teaching electron diffraction tube: an electron gun, a thin film of graphite crystals at the end of the anode, and a fluorescent screen on the inside of a glass bulb. You set the accelerating voltage ({#} to {#} kV) and read it on the kV meter, which jitters by a few volts like a real one.",
"Two rings appear. Ruler lays a flexible ruler on the glass through the central spot; it reads the arc across a ring to {#} mm. Caliper holds a vernier caliper in front of the screen; it reads the straight chord to {#} mm. Pick the ring (inner \\(D_1\\) or outer \\(D_2\\)) and drag the two marks or jaws onto the middle of the glowing band on each side.",
"A bar magnet can be held near the neck of the tube, with either pole facing it.",
"Record reading stores the meter reading and your \\(D_1\\) and \\(D_2\\), with the tool. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the Data table below; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table. Blind hides the wavelength worked out from the meter reading, the accepted spacings, the true ring positions and the angles. Tube seed fixes the small simulated imperfections, so a class can share one tube.",
"The X-rays button turns the demo into an X-ray spectrometer, used in the X-Ray Diffraction worksheet.",
"<strong>What the demo models.</strong> The tube follows the maker’s data sheet of a common teaching tube: graphite spacings {#} nm and {#} nm, graphite to screen \\(L = 125\\) mm, bulb radius \\(R = 65\\) mm. The wavelength is \\(\\lambda = h/p\\) with the relativistic momentum, and each ring is where the cone of electrons Bragg reflected at \\(2\\theta\\) meets the spherical screen, found exactly. The ring widths and brightness, the small tilt and slow wander of the beam, the meter jitter and the magnet’s effect are modeled, not computed from first principles. Simulated data.",
"Show that electrons are diffracted like waves, and use the known spacings of the planes of atoms in graphite as a ruler to measure the electron’s wavelength at each voltage. Then test de Broglie’s relation \\(\\lambda = h/p\\) against your measurements, and find Planck’s constant from them.",
"De Broglie proposed that a particle of momentum \\(p\\) has a wavelength \\(\\lambda = h/p\\) (§{#}). An electron accelerated from rest through a voltage \\(V\\) has kinetic energy \\(eV\\), so \\(p = \\sqrt{2meV}\\) and",
"\\[\\lambda = \\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2meV} }, \\qquad \\text{or, with the small relativistic correction,} \\qquad \\lambda = \\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2meV\\left(1 + \\dfrac{eV}{2mc^2}\\right)} }.\\]",
"A crystal reflects a wave strongly from a set of parallel planes of atoms a distance \\(d\\) apart only when Bragg’s law holds (§{#}):",
"\\[2d\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda,\\]",
"where \\(\\theta\\) is the glancing angle between the beam and the planes. The reflected beam leaves at \\(2\\theta\\) to the incident beam. The graphite film is made of many tiny crystals turned every way, so the reflected electrons leave on a cone around the beam and make a ring on the screen. Each ring comes from a different spacing \\(d\\) (first order, \\(m = 1\\)).",
"For small angles, a ring of diameter \\(D\\) on a screen a distance \\(L\\) from the graphite has \\(\\tan 2\\theta = D/(2L)\\), and with \\(\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta\\) and \\(\\tan 2\\theta \\approx 2\\theta\\),",
"\\[D \\approx \\frac{2L}{d}\\,\\lambda, \\qquad \\text{so} \\qquad \\lambda \\approx \\frac{d\\,D}{2L}.\\]",
"On this tube’s spherical screen, a diameter measured as the arc along the glass follows this line to within {#} %. The spacings are known from X-ray diffraction: graphite is made of flat sheets of hexagonal rings of carbon with \\(a = 0.246\\) nm, and the two rings come from rows of atoms \\(d_1 = a\\sqrt{3}/2 = 0.213\\) nm and \\(d_2 = a/2 = 0.123\\) nm apart. A measured \\(D\\) therefore gives the electron’s wavelength.",
"If de Broglie is right, then (without the small relativistic correction) \\(\\lambda = \\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2me} }\\,\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{V} }\\): a graph of \\(\\lambda\\) against \\(1/\\sqrt{V}\\) is a straight line through the origin with slope \\(h/\\sqrt{2me}\\).",
"Find the electron wavelength at {#} kV and at {#} kV, with and without the relativistic correction. By what percentage does the correction change \\(\\lambda\\)?",
"As the voltage rises, should the rings grow or shrink? By what factor should \\(D\\) change from {#} kV to {#} kV?",
"Why does a film of many small crystals turned every way give rings rather than spots?",
